<title>USA: U.S. 1997 acreage estimates--USDA.</title>
<headline>U.S. 1997 acreage estimates--USDA.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-06-30</dateline>
<text>
<p>The U.S. Agriculture Department on Monday estimated 1997 soybean plantings at 70.9 million acres, the largest since a matching 70.9 million acres in 1982 and the third highest on record.</p>
<p>In its first 1997 corn and soybean estimates based on field surveys taken in the first two weeks of June, USDA pegged corn plantings at 80.2 million acres, the largest since 83.4 million in 1985.</p>
<p>In the Prospective Plantings report released March 31, USDA had pegged soybean acreage at 68.8 million acres and corn at 81.4 million acres.  </p>
<p>Analysts had expected soybean acreage to dip as farmers took advantage of favorable spring planting weather and sowed more corn. On average, analysts pegged soybean acres at 68.4 million acres and put corn plantings at 81.8 million acres.</p>
<p>In a separate report, USDA said June 1 soybean stocks totaled 499.5 million bushels, the lowest since 465 million bushels in 1989. Corn stocks stood at 2.49 billion bushels and wheat was 444.2 million bushels.</p>
<p>Analysts on average had forecast June 1 soybean stocks at 516 million bushels, corn stocks at 2.478 billion bushels and wheat stocks at 466 million bushels.  </p>
